Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• event_name : filter “first_open”, “session_start” (or any event that qualifies as “return”)  
• event_timestamp (INT64, micro‑seconds) : build Shanghai dates and calculate day / week differences  
• user_pseudo_id : unique anonymous user id for cohort / retention counting  
• event_date : partition pruning when reading single–day tables (helps performance)  

Typical conditions  
• event_name = 'first_open' (to create the cohort)  
• event_name IN ('session_start','user_engagement') (to decide that a user “came back”)  
• DATE(TIMESTAMP_MICROS(event_timestamp),"Asia/Shanghai")  
     BETWEEN '2018‑09‑01' AND '2018‑09‑07'  -- cohort window  
• week_index IN (1,2,3)                    -- subsequent weeks  

Below are ten progressively more complex BigQuery queries (LIMIT 20 each).  
Every query is wrapped in back‑ticks and preceded by a short comment.

```sql
--1. Distinct event names recorded on 1‑Sep‑2018
SELECT DISTINCT event_name
FROM `firebase-public-project.analytics_153293282.events_20180901`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
